The fragment below sorts an array in place and accumulates a running total:
```
int[] a = {3, 1, 4, 1, 5, 9, 2, 6};
int c = 0;
for (int i = 1; i < a.length; i++) {
    int key = a[i], j = i - 1;
    while (j >= 0 && a[j] > key) {
        a[j + 1] = a[j];
        j--;
    }
    a[j + 1] = key;
    c += j + 1;
}
```
What is the value of c once the outer loop has run to completion?

A method is written as follows.
```
int[] a = {4, 7, 2, 9};
int total = 0;
for (int i = 0; i <= a.length; i++) {
    total += a[i];
}
```
What actually happens when this method runs, and what is the smallest change that makes the sum correct?

A quicksort partition is written as follows, and the missing condition is what decides how the array is split.
```
int[] a = {4, 4, 4, 4, 4};
int pivot = a[a.length - 1];
int i = 0;
for (int j = 0; j < a.length - 1; j++) {
    if (a[j] /* FILL IN */) {
        int t = a[i]; a[i] = a[j]; a[j] = t;
        i++;
    }
}
int u = a[i]; a[i] = a[a.length - 1]; a[a.length - 1] = u;
```
Which condition keeps the split balanced when every key in the array is equal to the pivot?

A halving search is written as follows, with integer division truncating towards zero.
```
int[] a = {2, 5, 8, 12, 16, 23, 38, 56, 72, 91};
int lo = 0, hi = a.length - 1;
while (lo <= hi) {
    int mid = (lo + hi) / 2;
    if (a[mid] == 23) break;
    if (a[mid] < 23) lo = mid + 1; else hi = mid - 1;
}
```
The search is run for the value 23 and stops as soon as it finds it. Which index is examined on the third comparison?

A routine is meant to add up the running prefix sums of an array, that is, to add the first element, then the first two, then the first three, and so on.
```
int[] a = {2, 5, 3, 8};
int total = 0;
for (int x : a) {
    int running = 0;
    running += x;
    total += running;
}
```
What value does total actually hold, and what value was intended?

A routine scans a list once, keeping a current sum and a best sum. Whenever the current sum is negative it discards it and starts again from the value just read.
```
int[] a = {3, -2, 5, -1, 4, -6, 2};
int cur = 0, best = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length; i++) {
    if (cur < 0) cur = a[i]; else cur += a[i];
    if (cur > best) best = cur;
}
```
Run on the array shown, what value does best hold when the loop finishes?

A bubble sort with an early exit is written as follows.
```
int[] a = {1, 2, 3, 5, 4};
int passes = 0;
for (int i = 0; i < a.length - 1; i++) {
    boolean swapped = false;
    passes++;
    for (int j = 0; j < a.length - 1 - i; j++) {
        if (a[j] > a[j + 1]) {
            int t = a[j]; a[j] = a[j + 1]; a[j + 1] = t;
            swapped = true;
        }
    }
    if (!swapped) break;
}
```
How many times does the outer loop body execute before the loop finishes?

A merge routine is meant to join two already sorted arrays into one sorted array of the combined length.
```
int[] merge(int[] a, int[] b) {
    int[] out = new int[a.length + b.length];
    int i = 0, j = 0, k = 0;
    while (i < a.length && j < b.length) {
        if (a[i] <= b[j]) out[k++] = a[i++];
        else out[k++] = b[j++];
    }
    return out;
}
```
Called with a holding 1, 3, 5 and b holding 2, 4, what does it return, and what is missing?
